    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "WNDF-AV-000050 - Microsoft Defender AV must block Office communication application from creating child processes."
      info        : "This policy setting prevents Outlook from creating child processes while still allowing legitimate Outlook functions. This rule protects against social engineering attacks and prevents exploiting code from abusing vulnerabilities in Outlook. It also protects against Outlook rules and forms exploits that attackers can use when a user's credentials are compromised."
      solution    : 'Set the policy value for Computer Configuration >> Administrative Templates >> Windows Components >> Microsoft Defender Antivirus >> Microsoft Defender Exploit Guard >> Attack Surface Reduction >> Configure Attack Surface Reduction rules to \'Enabled\'.

Under the policy option \'Set the state for each ASR rule:\', then click \'Show\'.

Enter GUID \'26190899-1602-49e8-8b27-eb1d0a1ce869\' in the \'Value Name\' column.

Enter \'1\' in the \'Value\' column.'
      reference   : "800-171|3.13.13,800-171r3|03.13.13,800-53|SC-18(4),800-53r5|SC-18(4),CAT|II,CCI|CCI-001170,CSF|DE.CM-5,DISA_Benchmark|MS_Defender_Antivirus,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SC-18(4),NIAv2|SU3,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-278654r1192815_rule,STIG-ID|WNDF-AV-000050,Vuln-ID|V-278654"
      see_also    : "https://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/stigs/zip/U_MS_Defender_Antivirus_V2R9_STIG.zip"
      value_type  : POLICY_TEXT
      value_data  : "1"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Policies\Microsoft\Windows Defender\Windows Defender Exploit Guard\ASR\Rules"
      reg_item    : "26190899-1602-49e8-8b27-eb1d0a1ce869"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "WNDF-AV-000051 - Microsoft Defender AV must block abuse of exploited vulnerable signed drivers."
      info        : "This policy setting prevents an application from writing a vulnerable signed driver to disk. Vulnerable signed drivers can be exploited by local applications that have sufficient privileges to gain access to the kernel. Vulnerable signed drivers enable attackers to disable or circumvent security solutions, eventually leading to system compromise.

The Block abuse of exploited vulnerable signed drivers rule does not block a driver already existing on the system from being loaded."
      solution    : 'Set the policy value for Computer Configuration >> Administrative Templates >> Windows Components >> Microsoft Defender Antivirus >> Microsoft Defender Exploit Guard >> Attack Surface Reduction >> Configure Attack Surface Reduction rules to \'Enabled\'.

Under the policy option \'Set the state for each ASR rule:\', then click \'Show\'.

Enter GUID \'56a863a9-875e-4185-98a7-b882c64b5ce5\' in the \'Value Name\' column.

Enter \'1\' in the \'Value\' column.'
      reference   : "800-171|3.13.13,800-171r3|03.13.13,800-53|SC-18(4),800-53r5|SC-18(4),CAT|II,CCI|CCI-001170,CSF|DE.CM-5,DISA_Benchmark|MS_Defender_Antivirus,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SC-18(4),NIAv2|SU3,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-278655r1192816_rule,STIG-ID|WNDF-AV-000051,Vuln-ID|V-278655"
      see_also    : "https://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/stigs/zip/U_MS_Defender_Antivirus_V2R9_STIG.zip"
      value_type  : POLICY_TEXT
      value_data  : "1"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Policies\Microsoft\Windows Defender\Windows Defender Exploit Guard\ASR\Rules"
      reg_item    : "56a863a9-875e-4185-98a7-b882c64b5ce5"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "WNDF-AV-000052 - Microsoft Defender AV must configure local administrator merge behavior for lists."
      info        : "This policy setting configures how locally defined lists are combined or merged with globally defined lists. This setting applies to exclusion lists, specified remediation lists, and attack surface reduction."
      solution    : 'Set the policy value for Computer Configuration >> Administrative Templates >> Windows Components >> Microsoft Defender Antivirus >> Configure local administrator merge behavior for lists to \'Enabled\'.'
      reference   : "800-171|3.13.13,800-171r3|03.13.13,800-53|SC-18(4),800-53r5|SC-18(4),CAT|II,CCI|CCI-001170,CSF|DE.CM-5,DISA_Benchmark|MS_Defender_Antivirus,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SC-18(4),NIAv2|SU3,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-278656r1192817_rule,STIG-ID|WNDF-AV-000052,Vuln-ID|V-278656"
      see_also    : "https://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/stigs/zip/U_MS_Defender_Antivirus_V2R9_STIG.zip"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "1"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Policies\Microsoft\Windows Defender"
      reg_item    : "DisableLocalAdminMerge"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "WNDF-AV-000055 - Microsoft Defender AV must randomize scheduled task times."
      info        : "In Microsoft Defender Antivirus, randomize the start time of the scan to any interval from 0 to 23 hours. By default, scheduled tasks begin at a random time within four hours of the time specified in Task Scheduler."
      solution    : 'Set the policy value for Computer Configuration >> Administrative Templates >> Windows Components >> Microsoft Defender Antivirus >> Randomize scheduled task times to \'Enabled\'.'
      reference   : "800-171|3.14.2,800-171|3.14.4,800-171|3.14.5,800-171r3|03.14.02c.01,800-53|SI-3c.1.,800-53r5|SI-3c.1.,CAT|II,CCI|CCI-002624,CN-L3|7.1.3.6(b),CN-L3|8.1.4.5,CN-L3|8.1.9.6(a),CN-L3|8.1.9.6(b),CN-L3|8.1.10.5(b),CN-L3|8.1.10.7(a),CN-L3|8.1.10.7(b),CSF|DE.CM-4,CSF|DE.DP-3,CSF2.0|PR.DS-01,CSF2.0|PR.DS-02,CSF2.0|PR.DS-10,DISA_Benchmark|MS_Defender_Antivirus,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.7,ISO/IEC-27001|A.12.2.1,ITSG-33|SI-3c.a.,NESA|T3.4.1,NIAv2|NS24,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|5.1,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|5.1.1,PCI-DSSv4.0|5.3.2,PCI-DSSv4.0|5.3.3,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|5.2.3,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-278659r1192819_rule,STIG-ID|WNDF-AV-000055,TBA-FIISB|49.2.1,TBA-FIISB|49.2.2,TBA-FIISB|49.3.1,TBA-FIISB|49.3.2,TBA-FIISB|50.2.1,TBA-FIISB|51.2.4,TBA-FIISB|51.2.7,Vuln-ID|V-278659"
      see_also    : "https://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/stigs/zip/U_MS_Defender_Antivirus_V2R9_STIG.zip"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "1"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Policies\Microsoft\Windows Defender"
      reg_item    : "RandomizeScheduleTaskTimes"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "WNDF-AV-000056 - Microsoft Defender AV must hide the Family options area."
      info        : "The Family options section contains links to settings and further information for parents of a Windows PC. It is not intended for enterprise or business environments.

This section can be hidden from users of the machine. This option can be useful if you do not want users in the organization to see or have access to this section."
      solution    : 'Set the policy value for Computer Configuration >> Administrative Templates >> Windows Components >> Windows Security >> Family Options >> Hide the Family options area to \'Enabled\'.'
      reference   : "800-171|3.13.13,800-171r3|03.13.13,800-53|SC-18(4),800-53r5|SC-18(4),CAT|II,CCI|CCI-001170,CSF|DE.CM-5,DISA_Benchmark|MS_Defender_Antivirus,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SC-18(4),NIAv2|SU3,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-278660r1192820_rule,STIG-ID|WNDF-AV-000056,Vuln-ID|V-278660"
      see_also    : "https://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/stigs/zip/U_MS_Defender_Antivirus_V2R9_STIG.zip"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "1"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Policies\Microsoft\Windows Defender Security Center\Family Options"
      reg_item    : "UILockdown"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "WNDF-AV-000057 - Microsoft Defender AV must enable the file hash computation feature."
      info        : "This policy drives the ability to enforce Indicators of Compromise (IoC) by using file hash allow/block indicators."
      solution    : 'Set the policy value for Computer Configuration >> Administrative Templates >> Windows Components >> Microsoft Defender Antivirus >> MpEngine >>Enable file hash computation feature to \'Enabled\'.'
      reference   : "800-171|3.13.13,800-171r3|03.13.13,800-53|SC-18(4),800-53r5|SC-18(4),CAT|II,CCI|CCI-001170,CSF|DE.CM-5,DISA_Benchmark|MS_Defender_Antivirus,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SC-18(4),NIAv2|SU3,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-278661r1192821_rule,STIG-ID|WNDF-AV-000057,Vuln-ID|V-278661"
      see_also    : "https://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/stigs/zip/U_MS_Defender_Antivirus_V2R9_STIG.zip"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "1"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Policies\Microsoft\Windows Defender\MpEngine"
      reg_item    : "EnableFileHashComputation"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "WNDF-AV-000058 - Microsoft Defender AV must enable extended cloud check."
      info        : "When Microsoft Defender Antivirus finds a suspicious file, it can prevent the file from running while it queries the Microsoft Defender Antivirus cloud service.

The default period that the file is blocked is 10 seconds. Extending the cloud block timeout period can help ensure there is enough time to receive a proper determination from the Microsoft Defender Antivirus cloud service."
      solution    : 'Set the policy value for Computer Configuration >> Administrative Templates >> Windows Components >> Microsoft Defender Antivirus >> MpEngine >> Configure extended cloud check to \'Enabled\' with a Policy Option value of \'50\'.'
      reference   : "800-171|3.13.13,800-171r3|03.13.13,800-53|SC-18(4),800-53r5|SC-18(4),CAT|II,CCI|CCI-001170,CSF|DE.CM-5,DISA_Benchmark|MS_Defender_Antivirus,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SC-18(4),NIAv2|SU3,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-278662r1192822_rule,STIG-ID|WNDF-AV-000058,Vuln-ID|V-278662"
      see_also    : "https://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/stigs/zip/U_MS_Defender_Antivirus_V2R9_STIG.zip"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "50"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Policies\Microsoft\Windows Defender\MpEngine"
      reg_item    : "MpBafsExtendedTimeout"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "WNDF-AV-000064 - Microsoft Defender AV must enable script scanning."
      info        : "Always-on protection consists of real-time protection, behavior monitoring, and heuristics to identify malware based on known suspicious and malicious activities. These activities include events, such as processes making unusual changes to existing files, modifying or creating automatic startup registry keys and startup locations (also known as autostart extensibility points, or ASEPs), and other changes to the file system or file structure."
      solution    : 'Set the policy value for Computer Configuration >> Administrative Templates >> Windows Components >> Microsoft Defender Antivirus >> Real-time Protection >> Turn on script scanning to \'Enabled\'.'
      reference   : "800-171|3.14.2,800-171|3.14.4,800-171|3.14.5,800-171r3|03.14.02c.01,800-53|SI-3c.1.,800-53r5|SI-3c.1.,CAT|II,CCI|CCI-002624,CN-L3|7.1.3.6(b),CN-L3|8.1.4.5,CN-L3|8.1.9.6(a),CN-L3|8.1.9.6(b),CN-L3|8.1.10.5(b),CN-L3|8.1.10.7(a),CN-L3|8.1.10.7(b),CSF|DE.CM-4,CSF|DE.DP-3,CSF2.0|PR.DS-01,CSF2.0|PR.DS-02,CSF2.0|PR.DS-10,DISA_Benchmark|MS_Defender_Antivirus,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.7,ISO/IEC-27001|A.12.2.1,ITSG-33|SI-3c.a.,NESA|T3.4.1,NIAv2|NS24,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|5.1,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|5.1.1,PCI-DSSv4.0|5.3.2,PCI-DSSv4.0|5.3.3,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|5.2.3,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-278668r1192823_rule,STIG-ID|WNDF-AV-000064,TBA-FIISB|49.2.1,TBA-FIISB|49.2.2,TBA-FIISB|49.3.1,TBA-FIISB|49.3.2,TBA-FIISB|50.2.1,TBA-FIISB|51.2.4,TBA-FIISB|51.2.7,Vuln-ID|V-278668"
      see_also    : "https://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/stigs/zip/U_MS_Defender_Antivirus_V2R9_STIG.zip"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "0"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Policies\Microsoft\Windows Defender\Real-Time Protection"
      reg_item    : "DisableScriptScanning"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "WNDF-AV-000065 - Microsoft Defender AV must enable real-time protection and Security Intelligence Updates during OOBE."
      info        : "Always-on protection consists of real-time protection, behavior monitoring, and heuristics to identify malware based on known suspicious and malicious activities. These activities include events, such as processes making unusual changes to existing files, modifying or creating automatic startup registry keys and startup locations (also known as autostart extensibility points, or ASEPs), and other changes to the file system or file structure."
      solution    : 'Set the policy value for Computer Configuration >> Administrative Templates >> Windows Components >> Microsoft Defender Antivirus >> Real-time Protection >> Configure real-time protection and Security Intelligence Updates during OOBE to \'Enabled\'.'
      reference   : "800-171|3.14.2,800-171|3.14.4,800-171|3.14.5,800-171r3|03.14.02c.01,800-53|SI-3c.1.,800-53r5|SI-3c.1.,CAT|II,CCI|CCI-002624,CN-L3|7.1.3.6(b),CN-L3|8.1.4.5,CN-L3|8.1.9.6(a),CN-L3|8.1.9.6(b),CN-L3|8.1.10.5(b),CN-L3|8.1.10.7(a),CN-L3|8.1.10.7(b),CSF|DE.CM-4,CSF|DE.DP-3,CSF2.0|PR.DS-01,CSF2.0|PR.DS-02,CSF2.0|PR.DS-10,DISA_Benchmark|MS_Defender_Antivirus,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.7,ISO/IEC-27001|A.12.2.1,ITSG-33|SI-3c.a.,NESA|T3.4.1,NIAv2|NS24,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|5.1,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|5.1.1,PCI-DSSv4.0|5.3.2,PCI-DSSv4.0|5.3.3,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|5.2.3,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-278669r1192824_rule,STIG-ID|WNDF-AV-000065,TBA-FIISB|49.2.1,TBA-FIISB|49.2.2,TBA-FIISB|49.3.1,TBA-FIISB|49.3.2,TBA-FIISB|50.2.1,TBA-FIISB|51.2.4,TBA-FIISB|51.2.7,Vuln-ID|V-278669"
      see_also    : "https://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/stigs/zip/U_MS_Defender_Antivirus_V2R9_STIG.zip"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "1"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Policies\Microsoft\Windows Defender\Real-Time Protection"
      reg_item    : "OobeEnableRtpAndSigUpdate"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "WNDF-AV-000068 - Microsoft Defender AV must enable network protection to be configured into block or audit mode on Windows Server."
      info        : "Microsoft's Exploit Guard comprises several techniques to defend against phishing attacks and malware. These include controlled folder access, attack surface reduction, and network protection."
      solution    : 'Set the policy value for Computer Configuration >> Administrative Templates >> Windows Components >> Microsoft Defender Antivirus >> Microsoft Defender Exploit Guard >> Network Protection >> This settings controls whether Network Protection is allowed to be configured into block or audit mode on Windows Server to \'Enabled\'.'
      reference   : "800-171|3.13.13,800-171r3|03.13.13,800-53|SC-18(4),800-53r5|SC-18(4),CAT|II,CCI|CCI-001170,CSF|DE.CM-5,DISA_Benchmark|MS_Defender_Antivirus,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SC-18(4),NIAv2|SU3,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-278672r1192825_rule,STIG-ID|WNDF-AV-000068,Vuln-ID|V-278672"
      see_also    : "https://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/stigs/zip/U_MS_Defender_Antivirus_V2R9_STIG.zip"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "1"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Policies\Microsoft\Windows Defender\Windows Defender Exploit Guard\Network Protection"
      reg_item    : "AllowNetworkProtectionOnWinServer"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "WNDF-AV-000070 - Microsoft Defender AV must enable EDR in block mode."
      info        : "EDR in block mode allows Microsoft Defender Antivirus to take actions on post-breach, behavioral EDR detections. EDR in block mode is integrated with threat and vulnerability management capabilities."
      solution    : 'Set the policy value for Computer Configuration >> Administrative Templates >> Windows Components >> Microsoft Defender Antivirus >> Features >> Enable EDR in block mode to \'Enabled\'.'
      reference   : "800-171|3.13.13,800-171r3|03.13.13,800-53|SC-18(4),800-53r5|SC-18(4),CAT|II,CCI|CCI-001170,CSF|DE.CM-5,DISA_Benchmark|MS_Defender_Antivirus,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SC-18(4),NIAv2|SU3,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-278674r1192826_rule,STIG-ID|WNDF-AV-000070,Vuln-ID|V-278674"
      see_also    : "https://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/stigs/zip/U_MS_Defender_Antivirus_V2R9_STIG.zip"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "1"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Policies\Microsoft\Windows Defender\Features"
      reg_item    : "PassiveRemediation"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "WNDF-AV-000071 - Microsoft Defender AV must report Dynamic Signature dropped events."
      info        : 'Microsoft Defender Antivirus logs \'Dynamic Signature dropped\' events when it blocks or removes a file based on a dynamically updated signature, but the signature itself is dropped, meaning it was not fully processed or applied. This can indicate a potential issue with signature updates or the system\'s ability to handle them.'
      solution    : 'Set the policy value for Computer Configuration >> Administrative Templates >> Windows Components >> Microsoft Defender Antivirus >> Reporting >> Configure whether to report Dynamic Signature dropped events to \'Enabled\'.'
      reference   : "800-171|3.13.13,800-171r3|03.13.13,800-53|SC-18(4),800-53r5|SC-18(4),CAT|II,CCI|CCI-001170,CSF|DE.CM-5,DISA_Benchmark|MS_Defender_Antivirus,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SC-18(4),NIAv2|SU3,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-278675r1192827_rule,STIG-ID|WNDF-AV-000071,Vuln-ID|V-278675"
      see_also    : "https://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/stigs/zip/U_MS_Defender_Antivirus_V2R9_STIG.zip"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "1"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Policies\Microsoft\Windows Defender\Reporting"
      reg_item    : "EnableDynamicSignatureDroppedEventReporting"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "WNDF-AV-000072 - Microsoft Defender AV must scan excluded files and directories during quick scans."
      info        : "In Microsoft Defender Antivirus, when an exclusion for a file or folder is created, it will generally be skipped during both real-time protection and on-demand scans (including quick scans and full scans). However, with newer releases, the option exists to configure quick scans to include files and directories that are otherwise excluded from real-time protection."
      solution    : 'Set the policy value for Computer Configuration >> Administrative Templates >> Windows Components >> Microsoft Defender Antivirus >> Scan >> Scan excluded files and directories during quick scans to \'Enabled\'.'
      reference   : "800-171|3.14.2,800-171|3.14.4,800-171|3.14.5,800-171r3|03.14.02c.01,800-53|SI-3c.1.,800-53r5|SI-3c.1.,CAT|II,CCI|CCI-002624,CN-L3|7.1.3.6(b),CN-L3|8.1.4.5,CN-L3|8.1.9.6(a),CN-L3|8.1.9.6(b),CN-L3|8.1.10.5(b),CN-L3|8.1.10.7(a),CN-L3|8.1.10.7(b),CSF|DE.CM-4,CSF|DE.DP-3,CSF2.0|PR.DS-01,CSF2.0|PR.DS-02,CSF2.0|PR.DS-10,DISA_Benchmark|MS_Defender_Antivirus,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.7,ISO/IEC-27001|A.12.2.1,ITSG-33|SI-3c.a.,NESA|T3.4.1,NIAv2|NS24,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|5.1,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|5.1.1,PCI-DSSv4.0|5.3.2,PCI-DSSv4.0|5.3.3,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|5.2.3,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-278676r1192828_rule,STIG-ID|WNDF-AV-000072,TBA-FIISB|49.2.1,TBA-FIISB|49.2.2,TBA-FIISB|49.3.1,TBA-FIISB|49.3.2,TBA-FIISB|50.2.1,TBA-FIISB|51.2.4,TBA-FIISB|51.2.7,Vuln-ID|V-278676"
      see_also    : "https://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/stigs/zip/U_MS_Defender_Antivirus_V2R9_STIG.zip"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "1"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Policies\Microsoft\Windows Defender\Scan"
      reg_item    : "QuickScanIncludeExclusions"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "WNDF-AV-000073 - Microsoft Defender AV must set cloud protection level to High."
      info        : "Cloud protection in Microsoft Defender Antivirus delivers accurate, real-time, and intelligent protection. Cloud protection should be enabled by default."
      solution    : 'Set the policy value for Computer Configuration >> Administrative Templates >> Windows Components >> Microsoft Defender Antivirus >> MpEngine >> Select cloud protection level to \'Enabled\'.

Set policy value \'Select cloud blocking level\' to \'High blocking level\'.'
      reference   : "800-171|3.13.13,800-171r3|03.13.13,800-53|SC-18(4),800-53r5|SC-18(4),CAT|II,CCI|CCI-001170,CSF|DE.CM-5,DISA_Benchmark|MS_Defender_Antivirus,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SC-18(4),NIAv2|SU3,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-278863r1192833_rule,STIG-ID|WNDF-AV-000073,Vuln-ID|V-278863"
      see_also    : "https://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/stigs/zip/U_MS_Defender_Antivirus_V2R9_STIG.zip"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "2"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Policies\Microsoft\Windows Defender\MpEngine"
      reg_item    : "MpCloudBlockLevel"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "WNDF-AV-000074 - Microsoft Defender AV must convert warn verdict to block."
      info        : "If a site URL has an unknown or uncertain reputation, a toast notification presents the user with the following options:

- Ok: The toast notification is released (removed), and the attempt to access the site is ended.

- Unblock: The user has access to the site for 24 hours, at which point the block is reenabled. The user can continue to use Unblock to access the site until such time that the administrator prohibits (blocks) the site, thus removing the option to Unblock.

- Feedback: The toast notification presents the user with a link to submit a ticket, which the user can use to submit feedback to the administrator in an attempt to justify access to the site."
      solution    : 'Set the policy value for Computer Configuration >> Administrative Templates >> Windows Components >> Microsoft Defender Antivirus >> Network Inspection System >> Convert warn verdict to block to \'Enabled\'.'
      reference   : "800-171|3.13.13,800-171r3|03.13.13,800-53|SC-18(4),800-53r5|SC-18(4),CAT|II,CCI|CCI-001170,CSF|DE.CM-5,DISA_Benchmark|MS_Defender_Antivirus,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SC-18(4),NIAv2|SU3,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-278677r1192829_rule,STIG-ID|WNDF-AV-000074,Vuln-ID|V-278677"
      see_also    : "https://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/stigs/zip/U_MS_Defender_Antivirus_V2R9_STIG.zip"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "1"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Policies\Microsoft\Windows Defender\NIS"
      reg_item    : "EnableConvertWarnToBlock"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "WNDF-AV-000075 - Microsoft Defender AV must enable asynchronous inspection."
      info        : "Network protection includes performance optimization that allows block mode to asynchronously inspect long-lived connections, which might provide a performance improvement. This optimization can also help with app compatibility problems."
      solution    : 'Set the policy value for Computer Configuration >> Administrative Templates >> Windows Components >> Microsoft Defender Antivirus >> Network Inspection System >> Turn on asynchronous inspection to \'Enabled\'.'
      reference   : "800-171|3.13.13,800-171r3|03.13.13,800-53|SC-18(4),800-53r5|SC-18(4),CAT|II,CCI|CCI-001170,CSF|DE.CM-5,DISA_Benchmark|MS_Defender_Antivirus,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SC-18(4),NIAv2|SU3,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-278678r1192830_rule,STIG-ID|WNDF-AV-000075,Vuln-ID|V-278678"
      see_also    : "https://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/stigs/zip/U_MS_Defender_Antivirus_V2R9_STIG.zip"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "1"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Policies\Microsoft\Windows Defender\NIS"
      reg_item    : "AllowSwitchToAsyncInspection"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "WNDF-AV-000076 - Microsoft Defender AV must scan packed executables."
      info        : "This policy setting manages whether Microsoft Defender Antivirus scans packed executables. Packed executables are executable files that contain compressed code."
      solution    : 'Set the policy value for Computer Configuration >> Administrative Templates >> Windows Components >> Microsoft Defender Antivirus >> Scan >> Scan packed executables to \'Enabled\'.'
      reference   : "800-171|3.14.2,800-171|3.14.4,800-171|3.14.5,800-171r3|03.14.02c.01,800-53|SI-3c.1.,800-53r5|SI-3c.1.,CAT|II,CCI|CCI-002624,CN-L3|7.1.3.6(b),CN-L3|8.1.4.5,CN-L3|8.1.9.6(a),CN-L3|8.1.9.6(b),CN-L3|8.1.10.5(b),CN-L3|8.1.10.7(a),CN-L3|8.1.10.7(b),CSF|DE.CM-4,CSF|DE.DP-3,CSF2.0|PR.DS-01,CSF2.0|PR.DS-02,CSF2.0|PR.DS-10,DISA_Benchmark|MS_Defender_Antivirus,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.7,ISO/IEC-27001|A.12.2.1,ITSG-33|SI-3c.a.,NESA|T3.4.1,NIAv2|NS24,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|5.1,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|5.1.1,PCI-DSSv4.0|5.3.2,PCI-DSSv4.0|5.3.3,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|5.2.3,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-278679r1192831_rule,STIG-ID|WNDF-AV-000076,TBA-FIISB|49.2.1,TBA-FIISB|49.2.2,TBA-FIISB|49.3.1,TBA-FIISB|49.3.2,TBA-FIISB|50.2.1,TBA-FIISB|51.2.4,TBA-FIISB|51.2.7,Vuln-ID|V-278679"
      see_also    : "https://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/stigs/zip/U_MS_Defender_Antivirus_V2R9_STIG.zip"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "0"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Policies\Microsoft\Windows Defender\Scan"
      reg_item    : "DisablePackedExeScanning"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "WNDF-AV-000077 - Microsoft Defender AV must enable heuristics."
      info        : "Always-on protection consists of real-time protection, behavior monitoring, and heuristics to identify malware based on known suspicious and malicious activities."
      solution    : 'Set the policy value for Computer Configuration >> Administrative Templates >> Windows Components >> Microsoft Defender Antivirus >> Scan >> Turn on heuristics to \'Enabled\'.'
      reference   : "800-171|3.14.2,800-171|3.14.4,800-171|3.14.5,800-171r3|03.14.02c.01,800-53|SI-3c.1.,800-53r5|SI-3c.1.,CAT|II,CCI|CCI-002624,CN-L3|7.1.3.6(b),CN-L3|8.1.4.5,CN-L3|8.1.9.6(a),CN-L3|8.1.9.6(b),CN-L3|8.1.10.5(b),CN-L3|8.1.10.7(a),CN-L3|8.1.10.7(b),CSF|DE.CM-4,CSF|DE.DP-3,CSF2.0|PR.DS-01,CSF2.0|PR.DS-02,CSF2.0|PR.DS-10,DISA_Benchmark|MS_Defender_Antivirus,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.7,ISO/IEC-27001|A.12.2.1,ITSG-33|SI-3c.a.,NESA|T3.4.1,NIAv2|NS24,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|5.1,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|5.1.1,PCI-DSSv4.0|5.3.2,PCI-DSSv4.0|5.3.3,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|5.2.3,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-278680r1192832_rule,STIG-ID|WNDF-AV-000077,TBA-FIISB|49.2.1,TBA-FIISB|49.2.2,TBA-FIISB|49.3.1,TBA-FIISB|49.3.2,TBA-FIISB|50.2.1,TBA-FIISB|51.2.4,TBA-FIISB|51.2.7,Vuln-ID|V-278680"
      see_also    : "https://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/stigs/zip/U_MS_Defender_Antivirus_V2R9_STIG.zip"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "0"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Policies\Microsoft\Windows Defender\Scan"
      reg_item    : "DisableHeuristics"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "WNDF-AV-000100 - Microsoft Defender AV must control whether exclusions are visible to Local Admins."
      info        : "Disabled (Default): If this setting is not configured or disabled, local admins can see exclusions in the Windows Security App or via PowerShell.

Enabled: If this setting is enabled, local admins no longer see the exclusion list in Windows Security App or via PowerShell.

Note: Applying this setting will not remove exclusions, it only prevents them from being visible to local admins. This is reflected in Get-MpPreference."
      solution    : 'Set the policy value for Computer Configuration >> Administrative Templates >> Windows Components >> Microsoft Defender Antivirus >> Control whether exclusions are visible to Local Admins to \'Enabled\'.'
      reference   : "800-171|3.13.13,800-171r3|03.13.13,800-53|SC-18(4),800-53r5|SC-18(4),CAT|II,CCI|CCI-001170,CSF|DE.CM-5,DISA_Benchmark|MS_Defender_Antivirus,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SC-18(4),NIAv2|SU3,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-278658r1207388_rule,STIG-ID|WNDF-AV-000100,Vuln-ID|V-278658"
      see_also    : "https://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/stigs/zip/U_MS_Defender_Antivirus_V2R9_STIG.zip"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "1"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Policies\Microsoft\Windows Defender"
      reg_item    : "HideExclusionsFromLocalAdmins"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>
  </then>

